  "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{z}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{z}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{z}=x{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{z}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & =x{e}^{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
